### A Practical Pattern: Swapping Struct Fields

```xiom
type Pair[T] = {
  first: T;
  second: T;
}

fn Pair.swap_elements[T](self: &mut Pair[T]) {
  let temp = self.first;
  self.first = self.second;
  self.second = temp;
}
```

Hmm, with move semantics, even this might not work directly. Let's focus on the conceptual lesson — the idea that generic functions let you operate on any type:

```xiom
fn describe[T](item: &T, label: Str) {
  io.println(label + ": <value>");
}

fn main() -> Int {
  describe(&42, "The answer");
  describe(&"hello", "Greeting");
  describe(&true, "Is it working?");
  return 0;
}
```

### A Working Generic Pattern: Building a New Value

```xiom
fn duplicate[T](items: &Vec[T]) -> Vec[T] {
  let result: Vec[T] = Vec[T].new();
  let i = 0;
  while i < items.len() {
    let item = match items.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => continue };
    result.push(item);
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

This reads items by borrowing and pushes them — the key is that we're not moving, we're copying/borrowing through `.get()`.

